# Unit V - Surya Namaskar
INTRO_U5 = {
 "about": "<b>Surya</b> = Sun, <b>Namaskar</b> = salutation / respectful greeting. A flowing sequence of yoga postures joined with controlled breathing and concentration; traditionally also reverence for the Sun. Usually practised in the morning, but any suitable time works if you've left enough time after eating. Used as a warm-up and as a complete sequence — it moves arms, legs, spine, shoulders, hips and trunk.",
 "objectives": ["Flexibility and mobility throughout the body","Muscular strength and endurance","Coordinate breathing with movement","Body awareness, balance, concentration","Prepare the body for other asanas","Relaxation and mindful movement","A regular, disciplined practice"],
 "importance": "Combines forward bends, backward extensions, lunges, plank-like positions and inverted-V positions in one sequence. At a suitable pace it gives both mobility work and physical exercise. Traditionally also a symbolic expression of gratitude to the Sun, which sustains life on Earth.",
 "prep": ["Clean, non-slip yoga mat","Comfortable clothing for free movement","Well-ventilated space with enough room","Not right after a heavy meal — wait about 2–3 hours after a large meal","Start with gentle neck, shoulder, wrist, hip, knee and ankle movements","Learn correct alignment of each posture before speeding up","Coordinate each movement with breathing; don't hold the breath","Beginners: 2–4 comfortable rounds, then increase gradually"],
 "precautions": "Wrist, shoulder, knee or back injury → modify the sequence. Balance difficulty or medical conditions affecting exercise tolerance → professional guidance. Pregnant students → learn pregnancy-appropriate modifications from a qualified instructor, not the standard sequence. Stop for sharp pain, dizziness, chest discomfort or unusual shortness of breath.",
}

